# DESCRIPTION

The core is what a native GPU driver implements and what its clients are written on.
It has the shape of Loinnir (loinnir(2)) and nothing more, and names no graphics API.
It is Swift, tier 0.
A backend is a class that conforms to `GPUDevice` and is its own `GPUQueue`; there are two:

- `VulkanDevice` (graphics/core/vulkan) runs the core over Vulkan: Venus's under QEMU, or a host's own.
- `MaliDevice` (graphics/core/mali) runs it on a Mali GPU's command streams, through the `csf` capset of the GPU class (mali(3)), with MaliCore's recipes and the compiler's Valhall backend (valhallbackend(2)).

A program that wants Loinnir's whole library takes `Loinnir`, which picks the backend by what the GPU class offers: a GPU that offers `csf` gets `MaliDevice`; any other, `VulkanDevice` over Venus.

## Memory

`allocate` gives memory by what it is for: `.upload` is written by the CPU and read by the GPU, `.device` is the GPU's alone, `.readback` is written by the GPU and read by the CPU.
A `GPUMemory` has a 64-bit GPU `address`, which shaders follow, and for `.upload` and `.readback` a CPU `pointer` to the same bytes.
On the Mali backend `.readback` memory, and an image that is drawn into and copied out of and never copied into, are mapped cached by the CPU, so reading them is as fast as memory; the backend cleans and invalidates their caches when a submission whose last step is the CPU's is done, and in `wait(_:)`, so what the CPU reads after either is what the GPU wrote.
`free` and every `destroy` are safe at any time: the device keeps the resource until the work submitted before the call has finished.

## Images and the heap

An image has a format (`rgba8`, `bgra8`, `r8`, `bgra8srgb`), a size and a usage (`sampled`, `renderTarget`, `storage`, `copySource`, `copyDestination`).
The device picks its layout and tiling and tracks the layout itself: nothing at the interface names one.
Sampled images rest ready to sample.

There is one bindless heap for each device, of three arrays: textures, samplers and storage images.
The caller owns the indices: it writes a slot with `writeTexture`, `writeSampler` or `writeStorageImage`, and a shader names the slot.
A slot may be written again at any time; work already submitted keeps the descriptor it was submitted with.
`limits` says how many slots each array has.

## Pipelines

`makeGraphicsPipeline` takes SPIR-V for a vertex and a fragment stage, the colour formats and the blend (`opaque`, or source alpha over); `makeComputePipeline` takes one SPIR-V module.
The device's own compiler makes the pipeline.
Every pipeline has the one layout: the heap, and a 64-bit root pointer, which is the first eight bytes of the push constants, as a `PushConstant` block of one buffer reference.
A draw or a dispatch passes the root pointer, the GPU address of whatever the shaders read; they reach textures by the indices that data holds.
There is no vertex input state and no descriptor set to state.

## Command lists

A `GPUCommandList` is data: commands recorded with no device, and replayed by the backend at `submit`.
It has compute dispatches (direct and indirect), passes with dynamic rendering, draws (direct, indexed and indirect), copies between memory and images or between memory, barriers, and queries.
A pass is `beginRendering` with the attachments, each saying what happens to its contents at the pass's start (`load`, `clear` or `dontCare`) and end (`store` or `discard`), as a tiler needs before the pass begins; `endRendering`; and the draws between.
The viewport and scissor start as the whole of the first attachment.
A barrier names stages only (`compute`, `vertex`, `fragment`, `transfer`): the earlier stages' writes are visible to the later stages' reads and writes.
`validate` is the one place the rules are written, and every backend holds a list to it before it runs: a draw outside a pass, a dispatch inside one, a copy past its memory, a copy into an image that isn't a copy destination, a pass that never ends.
A list that breaks them is `.invalid`; a list that needs what the device can't do (`DeviceFeatures`) is `.unsupported`, and its words say what.

## The timeline

A device has one timeline: a 64-bit counter that only goes up, which the GPU class's counters are (counter(2)).
`submit` waits until the timeline reaches `wait` (0 for no wait) and sets it to `signal` when the list is done; `signal` must be greater than any value the device has signalled, and `wait` must be a value some earlier submission signalled (`TimelineRule` keeps these).
Lists on one queue run in the order submitted.
`completed` is the highest value reached and `wait(_:)` waits for one.
A `.readback` allocation's bytes are the CPU's to read once the timeline has reached the value the submission that wrote them signalled.

## Queries

`makeQuerySet` makes a set of timestamps (in ticks of `limits.timestampPeriodPicoseconds`) or of occlusion counts; `writeTimestamp` and `beginQuery` and `endQuery` fill it and `results` reads it after the work has completed.

## The Vulkan backend

`VulkanDevice` is Loinnir's Vulkan code behind the core: one device with dynamic rendering, buffer device addresses, descriptor indexing and timeline semaphores, and the layout transitions a list needs, made as it replays the list into a command buffer.
Memory is a buffer with a device address; the heap is one descriptor set (sampled images at binding 0, samplers at binding 1, 16 of them, and with `storageImages: true`, where the device offers the features, storage images at binding 2).
It has every feature of the core.
On the dev machine it opens the host's Vulkan, or ours over Venus and vtest when `TODHCHAI_VULKAN_LIBRARY` names `libtdvulkan.so`.

## The Mali backend

`MaliDevice` runs on one VM of a `csf` context: each allocation is a buffer bound at a page-aligned address, and a list becomes steps.
A compute dispatch and a render pass of its draws are MaliCore's recipes (ComputeDispatch and RenderPass) laid out in memory of the VM, with the pipelines compiled from SPIR-V when they are made (compute shaders may not name descriptor buffers: the core's layout is the heap and a root pointer).
A pass with no draw, and every copy, is done by the CPU, in order with the passes.
It runs one submission at a time: a submission starts when the one before it is done.
`MaliTransport` is what it needs of the GPU's streams interface; `CSFTransport` is that interface over the `csf` capset's client, and a test can give another.
What it can do now: memory by intent; linear `rgba8`, `bgra8`, `bgra8srgb` and `r8` images, which a render pass writes and the texture unit reads in the same layout; the heap's textures and samplers; compute pipelines and dispatches; graphics pipelines for `rgba8`, `bgra8` or `bgra8srgb` targets, and passes of up to sixteen draws (instanced draws included) whose attachment is cleared (or `dontCare`) at its start.
The heap has 4096 texture slots and 16 sampler slots.
`writeTexture` and `writeSampler` keep MaliCore's descriptors (malicore(2)) in the device's copy of the heap, the samplers first and then the textures, and a pass whose fragment shaders read the heap is given a copy of it as it stands when the pass is built, as table 1 of its resource table: a later write reaches later passes only.
A sampler has the filter (the same for magnifying and minifying), the address mode on all three axes, no mipmaps and a transparent black border.
Only the fragment stage reads the heap, with a texture array at binding 0 and a sampler array at binding 1 of set 0, as Loinnir's shaders do.
A pipeline's blend is the fixed-function stage's: `opaque` replaces the target's colour, `alpha` is source alpha over.
On a `bgra8srgb` target the stage decodes the destination, blends in light and encodes the result, and a clear is encoded too; the fragment shader's colour is light.
A draw of fewer than three vertices or of no instances makes nothing.
What it refuses, each with words: storage images and the heap's storage slots, textures in compute shaders and in the vertex stage, a one-channel render target, indirect and indexed draws and indirect dispatch, more than sixteen draws in a pass, a pass that loads its attachment, several attachments, a viewport or scissor but the whole target, a draw from a first vertex or instance, queries.

